Testing SLH-DSA sign/verify flow...

  SLH-DSA Signing Process:

  1. Generate randomness: 591736e43d7a9eb9...
  2. Compute R = PRF(sk_prf, opt_rand, msg): 778f13ba9dc40b56...
  3. Derive indices: tree=15, leaf=0
  4. Sign with FORS (few-time signature on message digest)
     - Uses 4 trees with 8 leaves each
  5. Sign FORS pk with hypertree
     - 2 layers of XMSS trees
     - Each tree has height 4
     - Uses WOTS+ at each tree layer
  6. Signature = R || FORS_sig || HT_sig

  SLH-DSA Verification Process:

  1. Parse signature: R, FORS_sig, HT_sig
  2. Recompute message digest from R, pk, msg
  3. Derive tree/leaf indices from digest
  4. Verify FORS signature -> get FORS pk'
  5. Verify hypertree signature on FORS pk'
     - Traverse up through 2 XMSS layers
     - Verify WOTS+ signature at each layer
     - Use auth path to compute tree root
  6. Check final root matches pk.root

  Sign/verify flow demonstration PASSED!

=== SLH-DSA Parameter Sets (NIST Standards) ===

Security Level 1 (128-bit):
  SLH-DSA-SHA2-128f: Fast, 17,088 byte signatures
  SLH-DSA-SHA2-128s: Small, 7,856 byte signatures
  SLH-DSA-SHAKE-128f/s: Same but with SHAKE

Security Level 3 (192-bit):
  SLH-DSA-SHA2-192f: 35,664 byte signatures
  SLH-DSA-SHA2-192s: 16,224 byte signatures

Security Level 5 (256-bit):
  SLH-DSA-SHA2-256f: 49,856 byte signatures
  SLH-DSA-SHA2-256s: 29,792 byte signatures

Tradeoffs:
  'f' variants: Faster signing, larger signatures
  's' variants: Slower signing, smaller signatures
  SHA2 variants: Faster on hardware-accelerated platforms
  SHAKE variants: More conservative, simpler security proof

